            "title": "Build the series rules, work a supplied example, then solve one changed series circuit independently",
            "activityType": "Guided concept build with independent changed problem and explanation",
            "purpose": "Establish the accepted capability directly: correct, unassisted solution and explanation of one changed simple series circuit, using concrete supplied example data.",
            "instructions": "Use only two ideas plus the series rules below. If you have not been taught a different circuit method (for example nodal or mesh analysis), do not use one; the method required here is Ohm's law applied to a series circuit.\n\nA. Build the rules (about 15 minutes)\n1. Ohm's law for a resistor: V = I x R, so I = V / R and R = V / I.\n2. Series rules: the same current flows through every element in a single loop; the source voltage is shared across the resistors; resistances add, so R_total = R1 + R2; and the voltage drops add back to the source voltage: V1 + V2 = V_source.\nWrite these four facts in your own words once.\n\nB. Worked example you copy by hand (about 15 minutes)\nCircuit: a 12 V source in series with R1 = 100 ohm and R2 = 200 ohm.\nStep 1: R_total = 100 + 200 = 300 ohm.\nStep 2: I = V_source / R_total = 12 / 300 = 0.04 A = 40 mA.\nStep 3: V1 = I x R1 = 0.04 x 100 = 4 V; V2 = I x R2 = 0.04 x 200 = 8 V.\nStep 4: Check: 4 V + 8 V = 12 V, which matches the source. This sum check is the only check you need.\nRedo all four steps yourself on paper with the units written at each line.\n\nC. Changed circuit, no help (about 15-30 minutes)\nChoose your own simple series circuit: one DC source voltage between 6 V and 24 V, and exactly two resistors with values between 50 ohm and 500 ohm, different from each other and different from 100 ohm and 200 ohm. Then, without looking back at part B:\n1. Compute the total resistance, the current, and each voltage drop; write units on every quantity.\n2. Check that the two voltage drops add to the source voltage.\n3. In two or three sentences explain: why the current is the same through both resistors, which rule produced each quantity you calculated, and what would change in your numbers if you doubled R2 while keeping the source voltage the same.\n4. If part C cannot be completed without help, stop there and write down the exact step that failed or the exact sentence you could not justify. That record, not the time spent, is what the contingent repair step depends on.",
