            "purpose": "Replace an observed failure in the independent solve with correct unassisted performance on the same kind of circuit, without redoing steps that already worked.",
            "instructions": "Work on the failed step only, then re-demonstrate the capability.\n1. Write the step you recorded as failed and why it failed (for example: wrong R_total, treating the resistors as parallel, dividing instead of multiplying when finding a voltage drop, dropping units, or being unable to justify why the current is the same in both resistors).\n2. Repair only that point, using the same two rules from the main activity: V = I x R and R_total = R1 + R2, with the same current through both resistors.\n3. Redo just that step on the worked example (12 V; 100 ohm and 200 ohm) and check it against 0.04 A, 4 V and 8 V.\n4. Then choose one new changed two-resistor series circuit (source 6 V to 24 V; resistors 50 ohm to 500 ohm, different values from both your earlier attempt and the worked example), solve it fully with units, check that the drops sum to the source voltage, and explain each step in one or two sentences.\nDo not repeat parts of the main activity that were already correct and do not re-solve your earlier circuit.",
            "completionCriterion": "The previously failed step is performed correctly on the worked example, and the new changed two-resistor series circuit is solved correctly with units and the voltage-drop sum check, with each step explained using Ohm's law and the series rules, without help.",
